In any React Project, there is always an entry point HTML file, mostly named 'index.html'.
For any JS code to execute, it needs to be injected or linked to the HTML file.

In the HTML file, inside the body, there exists a div with any id (mostly 'root' in React). This div is where all our HTML will be injected.

The linked JS file first selects this predefined 'div' using its id and injects all the generated HTML for our project which then gets displayed on the actual webpage.

This is a reason why pure React applications are also called SPA (Single Page Applications) as all activities, page changes, element changes, rendering... etc happen in a single HTML file.

# What actually happens inside the JS file (index.js or main.jsx)?
    Docs: https://react.dev/reference/react-dom/client/createRoot
    1) ReactDOM: 
        For the purpose of optimizing re-rendering on websites/applications, many JavaScript frameworks provide various approaches. However, React's approach is the idea of the virtual DOM.
        React's virtual DOM implies a "virtual" representation (as a tree, as each element is a node that holds an object) of a user interface, which is preserved in memory and synchronized with the browser's DOM via React's ReactDOM library.

    2) createRoot():
        The createRoot() method is provided by ReactDOM and it is used to initialize the root node of the Virtual DOM tree. It takes a container element (our 'div' in the HTML file) as an argument and returns a ReactDOM root object.

    3)  render():
        The render() method is used by the root object returned by createRoot(). It accepts a single Component (JSX Div) that encloses all of the generated JSX code or sub components and makes updates on the webpage inside the selected 'root' div from 'index.html'.

