# Creating, Importing & Exporting components, JS vs JSX, Naming Conventions and Best Practices to follow:

    Components can be created in JS or JSX files. They are basically javascript functions that return HTML or JSX (Java Script XML). After creation, these functions are exported using the 'export' keyword.

    To include the components in another JS or JSX file, we first import them and then use them by their names as proper HTML tags. 
    E.g: 
    - Component file:
        function App() {
            return (
                <>
                    <h1>Hello World</h1>
                    <p>My App Component</p>
                </>
            )
        }
        export default App;
    - In Main JS file:
        import App from './App.js'
        ...
        // It can be now be used for rendering as:
        // <App />

    In a pure React project, the extension for the component files can be either '.js' or '.jsx'.
    However, in a Vite-React project, the extension for component files strictly needs to be '.jsx'.
    Note:
        Normal javascript code files that won't be used as components can always have the '.js' extension. (like files exporting hooks)
    
    Be it a pure React project or a Vite-React project, the components, that are to be used as markup tags, should always have a capital initial letter.
    E.g: <app /> - is not valid, <App /> - is valid.

    Irrespective of the build type of React project, these naming Conventions and Best Practices are to be followed:
    - Component File names and the name of the functional components inside them should always start with a Capital letter. (This helps the browser in differentiating between standard html tags and React components)
    - Always give the '.jsx' extension to your component files.


